Z||000000||TylerB11||10-25-2001||05:19 PM||||OK,<BR>I changed the fuel filter and now the car starts, and runs for 3-4 seconds (depending how many times i run he fuel pump alone) then dies.<P>I'm thinking fuel pump. Yeah or Nay?<P>EDIT: those of you who have changed it, can you give some pointers? can it be done with $16 ramps from wally world?<P>Thanks!<p>[This message has been edited by TylerB11 (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gary W||10-25-2001||09:24 PM||||Hot-wire the fuel pump to test it. Apply 12V DC to test pin G on the ALCL connector. On my '86, this is the pin closest to the driver on the top row. This will eliminate the oil pressure sending unit, fuel pump relay, and ECM from the circuit. Isolates the fuel pump. I'm working on a very similar problem.<P>Be absolutely sure what pins you're playing with before you mess with the ALCL<p>[This message has been edited by Gary W (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||samoht||10-25-2001||09:24 PM||||no problem.. get the back end up.  Disconnect the wiring harness, it run up the center on the fire wall.. the connection it like at the bottom of the fire wall..  there are 2 13mm (I think) T bolts that hold downmetal straps around the gas tank.  you will prolly have to replace these bolts.. lube um up real good, maybe you will get lucky..  Drop the gas tank down just far enough to unscrew the 3 hose clamps that hook the filler, vent and return hose. I would recommend replacing the return and the vent hoses at this time..  And walah..  the trick is gettin it back up....   it will be alot lighter if you run the gas out some how..  or have a freind help hold it up while taking and conecting these hoses..  it is more long and ackward than anything... good luck .. I put a corvette fuel pump in mine.. didnt work to do.. I think if you shop around you can get a 60PSI fuel pump for a few bucks over stock..  although you only need around 33psi at idle<P><BR>85GT3.2||||reg||1
